<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Søren Kierkegaard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Søren Kierkegaard was a Danish philosopher and theologian, regarded as the first existentialist. Writing often under pseudonyms, in works such as &quot;Either/Or&quot; (1843) and &quot;Fear and Trembling&quot; (1843), he explored faith, choice, anxiety and the individual's relation to God. In his last years he attacked the established Danish church. His ideas became widely influential in the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soren-kierkegaard.jpg" alt="Søren Kierkegaard" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: La Biblioteca Real de Dinamarca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%B8ren_Kierkegaard_(1813-1855)_-_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Søren Aabye Kierkegaard was born on 5 May 1813 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, the youngest of seven children of an affluent family. His father, Michael Pedersen Kierkegaard, was a devoutly Christian wool merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> who was devoted to the rationalist philosophy of Christian Wolff. The figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, whom Kierkegaard met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s dialogues, later shaped his interest in irony and indirect communication.</p><p>He met Regine Olsen on 8 May 1837 and proposed to her on 8 September 1840, after passing his theological examinations. He broke off the engagement on 11 August 1841, and after she pleaded with him he ended it verbally on 11 October 1841. His journals blame his melancholy, but his precise motive remains unclear, and the broken engagement is generally considered to have had a major influence on his work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Kierkegaard published some works under pseudonyms, each presenting a distinct viewpoint, as a way of indirect communication. Either/Or, considered his magnum opus, and Fear and Trembling, his central writing on religion, both appeared in 1843. Critics have accused him of playing with viewpoints without committing to one, though he said all his work came from a service to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p>His key ideas include subjective and objective truth, the knight of faith, anxiety, faith as a passion and the three stages on life's way. He wrote, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> teaches that the way is to become subjective, to become a subject.&quot; He attacked Hegelianism by parody, though he admired Hegel personally. A dispute that began with an article by Peder Ludvig Møller in December 1845 led him to ask the satirical paper The Corsair to satirise him. His journals run to over 7,000 pages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Kierkegaard attacked the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in newspaper articles and in a series of self-published pamphlets, The Moment. The attack began after Hans Lassen Martensen called the dead Bishop Jacob Peter Mynster a truth-witness, which Kierkegaard strongly objected to. He later said his earlier writing had been preparation for it. He collapsed in the street, spent over a month in Frederiks Hospital, refused communion, and died on 11 November 1855. His nephew Henrik Lund protested at the funeral against burial by the official church.</p><p>His work was written in Danish and was at first read mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, but was translated into French, German and other languages around the turn of the 20th century. Georg Brandes published the first book on his life and philosophy in 1879. His fame grew greatly in the 1930s, when the existentialist movement named him a precursor, and philosophers both theistic and atheistic drew from him the notions of angst, despair and the importance of the individual.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Søren Kierkegaard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Danish figures of wide influence include the philosopher <b>Søren Kierkegaard</b>, the writer Hans Christian Andersen and the physicist Niels Bohr, whose Copenhagen institute became a centre of quantum physics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Schopenhauer questioned earlier optimism, and <b>Søren Kierkegaard</b> and Friedrich Nietzsche rejected the idea that reality could be understood purely objectively.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Copenhagen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Søren Kierkegaard"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5601 5312h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5601 5312h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What is a poet? An unhappy man who conceals profound anguish in his heart, but whose lips are so fashioned that when sighs and groans pass over them they sound like beautiful music.&#8221;</p><footer>Either/Or (1843), Diapsalmata &#183; tr. Lee M. Hollander, 1923 &#183; from the Diapsalmata, written in the voice of the pseudonymous aesthete &quot;A&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60333/pg60333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let others complain that the times are wicked. I complain that they are paltry; for they are without passion. The thoughts of men are thin and frail like lace, and they themselves are feeble like girl lace-makers.&#8221;</p><footer>Either/Or (1843), Diapsalmata &#183; tr. Lee M. Hollander, 1923 &#183; from the Diapsalmata, written in the voice of the pseudonymous aesthete &quot;A&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60333/pg60333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My melancholy is the most faithful sweetheart I have had—no wonder that I return the love!&#8221;</p><footer>Either/Or (1843), Diapsalmata &#183; tr. Lee M. Hollander, 1923 &#183; from the Diapsalmata, written in the voice of the pseudonymous aesthete &quot;A&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60333/pg60333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Of all ridiculous things the most ridiculous seems to me, to be busy—to be a man who is brisk about his food and his work.&#8221;</p><footer>Either/Or (1843), Diapsalmata &#183; tr. Lee M. Hollander, 1923 &#183; from the Diapsalmata, written in the voice of the pseudonymous aesthete &quot;A&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60333/pg60333-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
